## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical dynamic connectivity under batch updates—a common scenario in real systems like messaging platforms (Discord, Slack) where friendship edges change in bursts. It evaluates understanding of amortized analysis, hybrid data structures, and the classic time-space trade-off in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- **Disjoint Set Union (DSU):** Provides near-constant time union-find with path compression and union by size
- **Batch processing:** Groups deletions to amortize costly operations across multiple updates
- **Threshold-based rebuilds:** Avoids accumulating O(n) drift from many deletions by periodically recomputing from scratch
- **BFS for local reconnection:** Only re-explores components affected by deletions, not the entire graph

## Code Example
```python
class DynamicComponents:
    def __init__(self, n, edges):
        self.n = n
        self.parent = list(range(n))
        self.size = [1] * n
        self.edges = set()
        for u, v in edges:
            self.add_edge(u, v)
        self.rebuild_threshold = int(n ** 0.5)

    def find(self, x):
        if self.parent[x] != x:
            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])
        return self.parent[x]

    def union(self, x, y):
        rx, ry = self.find(x), self.find(y)
        if rx == ry:
            return
        if self.size[rx] < self.size[ry]:
            rx, ry = ry, rx
        self.parent[ry] = rx
        self.size[rx] += self.size[ry]

    def add_edge(self, u, v):
        self.edges.add((min(u, v), max(u, v)))
        self.union(u, v)

    def process_batch(self, additions, deletions):
        for u, v in additions:
            self.add_edge(u, v)
        deletionsPerformed = 0
        for u, v in deletions:
            if (min(u, v), max(u, v)) in self.edges:
                self.edges.discard((min(u, v), max(u, v)))
                deletionsPerformed += 1
        if deletionsPerformed > self.rebuild_threshold:
            self.rebuild()
        return self.count_components()

    def rebuild(self):
        self.parent = list(range(self.n))
        self.size = [1] * self.n
        for u, v in self.edges:
            self.union(u, v)

    def count_components(self):
        return sum(1 for i in range(self.n) if self.find(i) == i)
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a stable online statistic with clear time/space guarantees using simple data structures.

## Key Concepts

- streaming algorithms
- two-heap median maintenance
- amortized vs worst-case costs
- space efficiency

## Code Example

```javascript
// production-ready sketch of two-heap median tracker
class BinaryHeap {
  constructor(compare){ this.data=[]; this.compare=compare; }
  size(){ return this.data.length; }
  top(){ return this.data[0]; }
  push(x){ this.data.push(x); this._siftUp(this.data.length-1); }
  pop(){ const top=this.data[0]; const end=this.data.pop(); if (this.data.length){ this.data[0]=end; this._siftDown(0); } return top; }
  _siftUp(i){ const x=this.data[i]; while(i>0){ const p=(i-1)>>1; if (this.compare(x, this.data[p])>=0) break; this.data[i]=this.data[p]; i=p; } this.data[i]=x; }
  _siftDown(i){ const n=this.data.length; const x=this.data[i]; while(true){ let l=2*i+1, r=l+1, smallest=i; if (l<n && this.compare(this.data[l], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=l; if (r<n && this.compare(this.data[r], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=r; if (smallest===i) break; this.data[i]=this.data[smallest]; i=smallest; } this.data[i]=x; }
}

class MedianTracker {
  constructor(){ this.lower=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> b-a); this.upper=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> a-b); }
  insert(x){ if (this.lower.size()===0 || x <= this.lower.top()) this.lower.push(x); else this.upper.push(x);
    if (this.lower.size() > this.upper.size()+1) this.upper.push(this.lower.pop());
    else if (this.upper.size() > this.lower.size()) this.lower.push(this.upper.pop());
  }
  median(){ if (this.lower.size() === this.upper.size()) return (this.lower.top() + this.upper.top())/2; return this.lower.top(); }
}
```

